    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Founded by Aragon’s conquest of Pisan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> in 1323–1324, it spent seven decades fighting the native Judicate of Arborea.</li><li>In 1720 the island and its royal title passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>, which ruled the kingdom from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, Savoy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>.</li><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a> it led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a> and in 1861 became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carta-de-logu">Carta de Logu</a>, issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-arborea">Eleonora of Arborea</a> in the late fourteenth century, remained in force as Sardinian law until 1827, long after Arborea itself had vanished.</p></aside>
    </section>

