    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii.jpg" alt="Pope Boniface VIII" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giotto_-_Bonifatius_VIII_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Giotto, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> began as a papal creation. In 1297 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a> granted a <em>Regnum Sardiniae et Corsicae</em> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-aragon">James II of Aragon</a>, partly as compensation for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>’s withdrawal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, but the grant had to be won by force. At the time Sardinia was divided between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Republic of Pisa</a>, which held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a> and the south-west with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines, Genoese noble families such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doria-family">Doria</a> in the north, and the native Judicate of Arborea in the west. In 1323 James’s son, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-iv-of-aragon">Infante Alfonso</a>, later Alfonso IV of Aragon, landed with Arborea as an ally and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iglesias">Villa di Chiesa</a> (Iglesias) and the Pisan fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-di-castro">Castel di Castro</a> above Cagliari. After an Aragonese victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lucocisterna">Lucocisterna</a> in 1324 Pisa ceded its Sardinian possessions, and the kingdom became a reality, joining the Crown of Aragon as one of its several realms. The Pisans were expelled from Cagliari altogether in 1326, and the town was resettled with Catalans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>, held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, was never conquered, and the claim to it remained a title only. Over the following century the new kingdom expanded slowly and violently from its southern and north-western footholds.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cagliari"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cagliari.jpg" alt="Cagliari" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cagliari_collage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nilo1926, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under Aragonese and Spanish rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> was a poor, thinly populated island organised on a feudal model. The crown granted most of the countryside as fiefs to Catalan, Aragonese and later Sardinian nobles, who drew dues from peasant communities living by grain farming and transhumant sheep and goat herding. The royal towns, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a>, Sassari, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alghero">Alghero</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iglesias">Iglesias</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oristano">Oristano</a>, enjoyed charters and their own councils. Alghero was repopulated with Catalans after a revolt in 1354, and a form of Catalan is still spoken there. Grain, salt, cheese, hides and coral were the main exports, carried largely by Catalan, Genoese and Ligurian merchants. Plague, malaria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> raids along the coasts kept the population low; a severe epidemic in 1652–1656 killed a large share of the inhabitants. Universities were founded at Sassari and Cagliari in the early seventeenth century. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a> the island remained distinct from the much richer mainland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> industry, wine and the growing city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>. Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-battista-bogino">Giovanni Battista Bogino</a> pushed reforms in Sardinia in the 1760s and refounded both universities, while feudal tenure on the island was abolished only in 1836–1838. The Catholic Church dominated religious life in both parts of the kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sanluri"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-sanluri.jpg" alt="Battle of Sanluri" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sanluri">Battle of Sanluri</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MarghinottibattagliaSanluri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Giovanni Marghinotti (1798-1865), CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom was born and defended in war. The struggle against the Judicate of Arborea, which turned against Aragon in 1353 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariano-iv-of-arborea">Marianus IV</a>, lasted most of seventy years; for long periods Arborea held almost the entire island, and the Aragonese kept little more than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alghero">Alghero</a>. The decisive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sanluri">Battle of Sanluri</a> in 1409, won by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-i-of-sicily">Martin the Younger</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, broke Arborean power, and the remaining rights were bought from William III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> in 1420. The last great revolt, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-alagon">Leonardo Alagon</a>, Marquis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oristano">Oristano</a>, was crushed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-macomer">Battle of Macomer</a> in 1478. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> the island’s defence rested on coastal towers built against North African corsairs. The Savoyard kingdom was a different military power: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> kept a disciplined standing army and fortresses guarding the Alpine passes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-emmanuel-iii-of-sardinia">Charles Emmanuel III</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-assietta">Battle of Assietta</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1747, but Napoleon’s Italian campaign forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-cherasco">Armistice of Cherasco</a> in 1796. In the Risorgimento the army, including the Bersaglieri light infantry created in 1836, was beaten by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-custoza-1848">Custoza</a> in 1848 and Novara in 1849. A contingent sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> in 1855 restored its standing, and with French help it won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magenta">Magenta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-solferino">Solferino</a> in 1859.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/carta-de-logu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carta-de-logu.jpg" alt="Carta de Logu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/carta-de-logu">Carta de Logu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pag1_carta_delogu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harlok83, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> was a separate kingdom governed in the king’s name by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a>, with a royal audience as high court from the sixteenth century. A parliament of three estates, the <em>Stamenti</em> (ecclesiastical, military or noble, and royal towns), met at intervals to vote the donative, the tax granted to the crown, and to present grievances. In the Arborean west, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carta-de-logu">Carta de Logu</a></em> issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-arborea">Eleonora of Arborea</a>, probably in 1392, stayed in force as Sardinian law until 1827. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1718">Treaty of London</a> of 1718 assigned the island to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II of Savoy</a> in exchange for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, the title of king passed in 1720 to a dynasty whose real centre was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>. The rulers of Savoy governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, Savoy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> and, after 1815, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a> as absolute monarchs through ministers and intendants, while Sardinia kept its viceroy and its separate laws. The so-called Perfect Fusion of 1847 ended this separation at the request of Sardinian elites. On 4 March 1848 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-albert-of-sardinia">Charles Albert</a> granted the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statuto-albertino">Statuto Albertino</a></em>, a constitution with an elected chamber, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a> and his prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Camillo Benso, Count of Cavour</a>, from 1852, the kingdom became a working parliamentary monarchy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/statuto-albertino"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/statuto-albertino.jpg" alt="Statuto Albertino" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/statuto-albertino">Statuto Albertino</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carlo_Alberto_firma_lo_Statuto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom’s lasting importance comes from two very different phases. In its Iberian centuries it tied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> to the Catalan and Spanish world: Catalan and Spanish shaped the island’s administrative language, its towns and its nobility, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alghero">Alghero</a> remains a Catalan-speaking enclave. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carta-de-logu">Carta de Logu</a></em> became a symbol of Sardinian identity, and the uprising of 28 April 1794 against Piedmontese officials is commemorated today as <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sa-die-de-sa-sardigna">Sa die de sa Sardigna</a></em>, Sardinia’s regional day. In its Savoyard phase the kingdom became the instrument of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II</a>’s royal title gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a> the rank it needed among European dynasties, and the liberal reforms after 1848 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> a refuge for exiles from other Italian states. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a>’s diplomacy, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> to the agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plombieres-agreement">Plombières</a> in 1858, drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> into war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and allowed the kingdom to absorb <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, the central Italian duchies and, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expedition-of-the-thousand">Expedition of the Thousand</a>, the south. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statuto-albertino">Statuto Albertino</a></em> became the constitution of united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and remained formally in force until 1948. The price of unification was the cession of Savoy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> to France in 1860.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-castelfidardo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-castelfidardo.jpg" alt="Battle of Castelfidardo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-castelfidardo">Battle of Castelfidardo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giovanni_Gallucci_La_Battaglia_di_Castelfidardo_palazzo_comunale_di_Castelfidardo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">it:Giovanni Gallucci (b. 1815 - d.: unknown), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> did not fall; it was transformed. Its low point came during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-emmanuel-iv-of-sardinia">Charles Emmanuel IV</a> was driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> in 1798 and the court took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a>; his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-i-of-sardinia">Victor Emmanuel I</a> lived there from 1806 until 1814. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> restored the mainland and added the former Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. After the defeat at Novara in 1849 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-albert-of-sardinia">Charles Albert</a> abdicated, but his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a> kept the constitution. The war of 1859 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-villafranca">Armistice of Villafranca</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zurich">Treaty of Zurich</a>; in 1860 plebiscites joined Tuscany, Parma, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a>, while Savoy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> were ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turin-1860">Treaty of Turin</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a> handed southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to Victor Emmanuel, whose troops had crossed the Papal States after defeating the papal army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-castelfidardo">Castelfidardo</a>. On 17 March 1861 the parliament in Turin proclaimed Victor Emmanuel II King of Italy. The new state kept the Sardinian constitution, army, administration and dynasty, and even its regnal numbering, and historians usually treat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> as the Kingdom of Sardinia renamed and enlarged. The island of Sardinia is today an autonomous region of the Italian Republic.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> began as an Aragonese conquest on a poor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> island and ended as the Savoyard state that unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>; its constitution, army and dynasty passed directly into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> in 1861.</em></p>

